= i
{parent=2f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**False.** If <uniform convergence> held, some <polynomial> $P_n$ would satisfy $\sup_{t\in\mathbb R}|P_n(t)-\sin t|<1$. Such a <polynomial> would be bounded on $\mathbb R$, hence constant: a nonconstant <polynomial> grows without bound in absolute value along a real half-line. For any constant $c$, however,
$$
\sup_{t\in\mathbb R}|c-\sin t|\geq\max\{|c-1|,|c+1|\}\geq1,
$$
a contradiction.

= ii
{parent=2f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**True.** By the <Weierstrass approximation theorem>, choose a <polynomial> $Q_n$ with $\sup_{|t|\leq n}|Q_n(t)-f(t)|<1/n$. For each fixed $t$, this bound applies once $n\geq|t|$, so $Q_n(t)\to f(t)$. This proves <pointwise convergence>; the same construction even gives <uniform convergence> on every <closed interval>.

= iii
{parent=2f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**True.** Define $h(u)=g(1/u)$ for $0<u\leq1$ and $h(0)=0$. The hypothesis at infinity makes $h$ <continuous> on $[0,1]$. The <Weierstrass approximation theorem> gives <polynomials> $R_n$ with $\sup_{0\leq u\leq1}|R_n(u)-h(u)|\to0$. Since $t\mapsto1/t$ maps $[1,\infty)$ onto $(0,1]$,
$$
\boxed{\sup_{t\geq1}|R_n(1/t)-g(t)|\longrightarrow0.}
$$

= 3G
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Coding & Cryptography}

= Solution
{parent=3G}

Take a code alphabet of size $D\geq2$ and prescribed positive <integer> lengths $\ell_1,\ldots,\ell_q$. A <decipherable code> means that concatenation of codewords determines the entire finite sequence of codewords uniquely. First prove the necessary <Kraft inequality>. If $A(m,L)$ counts sequences of $m$ codewords with total length $L$, <unique decodability> gives $A(m,L)\leq D^L$. Therefore
$$
K^m:=\left(\sum_{i=1}^qD^{-\ell_i}\right)^m
=\sum_{L=m\ell_{\min}}^{m\ell_{\max}}A(m,L)D^{-L}
\leq m(\ell_{\max}-\ell_{\min})+1.
$$
Taking $m$th roots and letting $m\to\infty$ gives $K\leq1$.

Conversely, order the lengths increasingly and construct a <prefix code> in the rooted $D$-ary <tree>. At depth $\ell_j$, previously selected codewords forbid exactly $\sum_{i<j}D^{\ell_j-\ell_i}$ vertices. The partial <Kraft inequality> implies
$$
\sum_{i<j}D^{\ell_j-\ell_i}\leq D^{\ell_j}-1,
$$
so an available vertex exists. Select it as the next codeword. Earlier lengths are no greater, so this preserves the <prefix code> property. A <prefix code> has <unique decodability>: in two purported parsings, the first unequal codewords would force one to be a prefix of the other. This proves the <equivalence of decipherable and prefix code lengths>: **the two existence conditions are equivalent**.

For a one-symbol alphabet, at most one positive-length codeword can be decipherable: two different unary codewords commute under concatenation. At most one is also exactly the prefix-code condition, so the equivalence still holds. The same argument covers countably many prescribed positive lengths for $D\geq2$: necessity applies to each finite subset, and $K\leq1$ ensures finitely many words at each length, allowing the ordered construction. Empty codewords are excluded, as required for <unique decodability> of arbitrary sequences.

= 4H
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Automata and Formal Languages}

= a
{parent=4h}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use $L=\{a^nb^n:n\geq0\}$. The <context-free grammar> $S\to aSb\mid\varepsilon$ generates exactly $L$, so it is a <context-free language>. It is not a <regular language>: if $p$ is a pumping length, any allowed decomposition of $a^pb^p$ has a nonempty pumped segment consisting only of $a$'s. Pumping down leaves fewer $a$'s than $b$'s, contradicting the <pumping lemma for regular languages>. Thus **$L$ is a <context-free language> but not a <regular language>**.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use $L=\{a^nb^nc^n:n\geq0\}$. A halting algorithm checks that a word consists of three consecutive blocks in the correct order and compares their lengths, so $L$ is a <decidable language>. If it were a <context-free language>, apply the <pumping lemma for context-free languages> to $a^pb^pc^p$. The segment containing both pumped pieces has length at most $p$, so it cannot meet both the $a$ and $c$ blocks. Pumping down therefore changes at least one block count but leaves another equal to $p$, or destroys the required block order. Either way the resulting word is not in $L$, a contradiction. Thus **$L$ is a <decidable language> but not a <context-free language>**.

= b
{parent=4h}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Fix an effective numbering of <Turing machines> and use the <diagonal halting set>
$$
H=\{e:M_e(e)\text{ halts}\}.
$$
It is <computably enumerable>: dovetail all the computations and output each index when its computation halts. It is not a <recursive set>. Otherwise a machine could, on input $e$, loop if the alleged decider says $e\in H$ and halt otherwise. Applying this machine to its own index contradicts either answer. Hence **$H$ is <computably enumerable> but not a <recursive set>**.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

With the <diagonal halting set> $H$, define
$$
\boxed{A=\{2e:e\in H\}\cup\{2e+1:e\notin H\}.}
$$
If $A$ were <computably enumerable>, its odd-index inverse image would make $\mathbb N\setminus H$ <computably enumerable>. If $\mathbb N\setminus A$ were <computably enumerable>, its even-index inverse image would do the same. But a set and its complement being <computably enumerable> would make the set a <recursive set>: run the two enumerations in parallel until the input appears in one. This contradicts the undecidability of $H$. Thus **neither $A$ nor its complement is <computably enumerable>**.

= 5J
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Modelling}

= a
{parent=5j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the usual <normal linear model> $Y=X\beta+\varepsilon$, with fixed full-column-rank design $X\in\mathbb R^{n\times p}$, $n>p$, and $\varepsilon\sim N(0,\sigma^2I_n)$. These <Gaussian distribution> and constant-<variance> hypotheses are necessary for an exact finite-sample <Student's t-test>. Put $V=(X^TX)^{-1}$ and $\nu=n-p$. The vector of <ordinary least squares estimators> satisfies
$$
\widehat\beta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY,\qquad\boxed{\widehat\beta_0\sim N(\beta_0,\sigma^2V_{00}).}
$$
With $s^2=\|Y-X\widehat\beta\|^2/\nu$, the <test statistic> is
$$
\boxed{T=\frac{\widehat\beta_0}{s\sqrt{V_{00}}}.}
$$
Under the <null hypothesis>, $T$ has <Student's t-distribution> with $\nu$ <degrees of freedom>. A two-sided level-$5\%$ <Student's t-test> rejects when $|T|>t_{\nu,0.975}$. If the intended test is one-sided against $\beta_0>0$, it rejects when $T>t_{\nu,0.95}$; the <statistical power> conclusion below holds for either convention.

= b
{parent=5j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <orthogonal projections> of the <Gaussian noise> onto the column space of $X$ and its <orthogonal complement> are <independent>. Consequently $\widehat\beta_0$ and the residual sum of squares are <independent>, and
$$
Z=\frac{\widehat\beta_0-\beta_0}{\sigma\sqrt{V_{00}}}\sim N(0,1),\qquad
U=\frac{\nu s^2}{\sigma^2}\sim\chi^2_\nu.
$$
Thus under the alternative,
$$
\boxed{T=\frac{Z+\delta}{\sqrt{U/\nu}},\qquad\delta=\frac{\beta_0}{\sigma\sqrt{V_{00}}},\quad Z\perp U.}
$$
This is the <noncentral t-distribution>. The rejection region is fixed by the design, $\nu$ and the chosen level, while this <probability distribution> depends on $\beta$ only through $\beta_0$. Therefore **the <statistical power> does not depend on the other regression coefficients**, with the design and $\sigma$ fixed.

= 6B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= Solution
{parent=6B}

For this <chemical reaction network>, the <law of mass action> gives the reaction rates are $k_1s^2e$, $k_2c$ and $k_3c$. Their <stoichiometry> gives
$$
\dot s=-2k_1s^2e+2k_2c,\quad
\dot e=-k_1s^2e+(k_2+k_3)c,\quad
\dot c=k_1s^2e-(k_2+k_3)c,\quad
\dot p=2k_3c.
$$
Here the convention for $k_1$ absorbs any combinatorial factor for two identical reactants. Direct differentiation gives the two <conservation laws>
$$
\boxed{e+c=e_0,\qquad s+2c+p=s_0.}
$$
Eliminating $e$ and $p$ leaves
$$
\dot s=-2k_1s^2(e_0-c)+2k_2c,\qquad
\dot c=k_1s^2(e_0-c)-(k_2+k_3)c.
$$
For positive $s_0,e_0,k_1$, use the <dimensionless variables> $u=s/s_0$, $v=c/e_0$ and $\tau=k_1e_0s_0t$. With prime marks denoting differentiation in $\tau$, this becomes
$$
\boxed{u'=-2u^2(1-v)+2(\mu-\lambda)v,\qquad\epsilon v'=u^2(1-v)-\mu v,}
$$
where
$$
\boxed{\epsilon=\frac{e_0}{s_0},\quad\mu=\frac{k_2+k_3}{k_1s_0^2},\quad\lambda=\frac{k_3}{k_1s_0^2},\quad u(0)=1,\quad v(0)=0.}
$$

= 7E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= Solution
{parent=7E}

The given <Euler product for the gamma function> has the finite approximants
$$
\Gamma_m(z)=\frac{m!(m+1)^z}{z(z+1)\cdots(z+m)},\qquad\Gamma_m(z)\longrightarrow\Gamma(z)
$$
away from the poles. Split the denominator of $\Gamma_{2m+1}(2z)$ into even and odd factors. The resulting exact identity is
$$
\frac{\Gamma_{2m+1}(2z)}{2^{2z}\Gamma_m(z)\Gamma_m(z+\tfrac12)}
=\frac{(2m+1)!}{2^{2m+2}(m!)^2\sqrt{m+1}}.
$$
The right side does not depend on $z$, so its <limit> is a constant $C$. Evaluate the ratio at $z=1/2$: $\Gamma(1)=1$, and the <gamma reflection formula>, together with positivity on the positive real axis, gives $\Gamma(1/2)=\sqrt\pi$. Hence
$$
\boxed{C=\frac1{2\sqrt\pi},\qquad\Gamma(2z)=\frac{2^{2z-1}}{\sqrt\pi}\Gamma(z)\Gamma(z+\tfrac12).}
$$
This is the <gamma duplication formula>. The argument first applies where the factors are finite; the identity then holds as an identity of <meromorphic functions>, with values at poles interpreted accordingly.

= 8E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= Solution
{parent=8E}

For variations vanishing at both endpoints, differentiating the <action> and using <integration by parts> gives
$$
\delta S=\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\sum_i\left(\frac{\partial L}{\partial q_i}-\frac{d}{dt}\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_i}\right)\delta q_i\,dt.
$$
The <fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations> therefore yields the <Euler-Lagrange equations>
$$
\boxed{\frac{d}{dt}\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_i}=\frac{\partial L}{\partial q_i}.}
$$
For the specified <Lagrangian>, the <gauge transformation> changes it by
$$
\Delta L=\dot{\mathbf r}\cdot\nabla F-\alpha F_t
=\frac{dF}{dt}-(\alpha+1)F_t.
$$
For arbitrary $F$, it is a <total derivative> precisely when $\boxed{\alpha=-1}$. The corresponding change in <action> is then an endpoint term whose variation is zero. In general time-dependent notation, the equations are
$$
m\ddot{\mathbf r}=-\nabla\phi-\partial_t\mathbf A+\dot{\mathbf r}\times(\nabla\times\mathbf A).
$$
The <electric field> $-\nabla\phi-\partial_t\mathbf A$ and <magnetic field> $\nabla\times\mathbf A$ are unchanged by the <gauge transformation> with $\alpha=-1$. Although the original potentials have no time dependence, their transformed versions generally do; allowing this is essential to the stated invariance.

= 9C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= Solution
{parent=9C}

Differentiation of the <Hubble parameter> gives $\dot H+H^2=\ddot a/a$. The <strong energy condition> and the acceleration equation therefore imply
$$
\boxed{\dot H+H^2\leq0.}
$$
Assume the present universe is expanding, $H_0>0$, and normalize the <scale factor> by $a(t_0)=1$. Since $\ddot a\leq0$, the past expanding branch has $\dot a(t)\geq\dot a(t_0)>0$. On this branch,
$$
\frac{d}{dt}\frac1H=-\frac{\dot H}{H^2}\geq1.
$$
Integrating from a past time $t$ to $t_0$ gives $H(t)^{-1}\leq H_0^{-1}+t-t_0$. Thus, where the denominator is positive,
$$
\boxed{H(t)\geq\frac1{H_0^{-1}+t-t_0}\quad(t\leq t_0).}
$$
The printed inequality needs this past-time qualification: for future times with $H>0$, the inequality reverses. Independently, a <concave function> lies below its tangent, so
$$
\boxed{a(t)\leq1+H_0(t-t_0).}
$$
Positivity of $a$ prevents the past branch extending before the zero of this tangent. If $t_B$ is the beginning of the expanding universe, its age satisfies $\boxed{t_0-t_B\leq H_0^{-1}}$. Without the normalization $a(t_0)=1$, the tangent bound instead reads $a(t)\leq a(t_0)[1+H_0(t-t_0)]$.

= 10F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Topics In Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=10F}

The <Baire category theorem> says that in a nonempty <complete metric space>, every countable intersection of open dense sets is dense. To prove it, start in an arbitrary nonempty <open set> $O$ and let $G_n$ be open dense. Inductively choose closed balls $\overline B(x_n,r_n)$ with $0<r_n\leq2^{-n}$, contained in $G_n$ and in the interior of the previous ball, with the first also contained in $O$. Density provides a point at each stage, and openness permits the smaller closed ball. The centers are a <Cauchy sequence>; <completeness> supplies a <limit> lying in every closed ball, hence in $O\cap\bigcap_nG_n$. The proof also works at isolated points, where sufficiently small balls are singletons.

The incomplete <metric space> $\mathbb Q$ is a counterexample: it is the countable union of its closed nowhere dense singletons, so the complements are open dense with empty intersection.

For the sequence in the problem, put
$$
E_N=\bigcap_{n,m\geq N}\{x\in[0,1]:|f_n(x)-f_m(x)|\leq\epsilon\}.
$$
Each $E_N$ is closed by <continuity>. <Pointwise convergence> makes each point's values a <Cauchy sequence>, so $[0,1]=\bigcup_NE_N$. By the <Baire category theorem>, one $E_N$ has nonempty relative interior, which contains a nonempty open interval $J$ in $(0,1)$. This proves the required common tail bound.

If <continuous> $g_n$ converged pointwise to the rationality indicator, take $\epsilon=1/4$ and let $m\to\infty$ in this bound. On $J$, $|g_n-g|\leq1/4$ for $n\geq N$. Hence a fixed <continuous> $g_N$ is at least $3/4$ at every rational point and at most $1/4$ at every irrational point of $J$. The density of both sets contradicts <continuity>. Thus **the rationality indicator is not a pointwise <limit> of <continuous> functions**.

Nevertheless $h_n(x)=x^n$ are <continuous> and converge pointwise to $h(x)=0$ for $x<1$, $h(1)=1$. This <limit> is discontinuous at $1$: **a pointwise <limit> of <continuous> functions can be discontinuous**.

= 11G
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Coding & Cryptography}

= Solution
{parent=11G}

For a discrete <random variable> with probabilities $p_i$, its <Shannon entropy> in bits is $H(X)=-\sum_ip_i\log_2p_i$, with $0\log_20=0$. <Gibbs inequality> states that
$$
D(p\Vert q)=\sum_ip_i\log_2\frac{p_i}{q_i}\geq0,
$$
with equality exactly when $p=q$. If $q_i=0<p_i$, the left side is infinite. Otherwise apply $-\ln u\geq1-u$ to $u=q_i/p_i$ for $p_i>0$:
$$
(\ln2)D(p\Vert q)\geq\sum_{p_i>0}(p_i-q_i)\geq0.
$$
Equality requires $q_i=p_i$ on the support and no remaining mass outside it. In particular, comparison with the uniform <probability distribution> on two outcomes gives binary <Shannon entropy> at most $1$.

Write $q=1-p_1$. For $q>0$, grouping the last two outcomes gives
$$
H(p_1,p_2,p_3)=H(p_1,q)+qH(p_2/q,p_3/q)\leq H(p_1,q)+q.
$$
Equality holds if $p_2=p_3$; if $q=0$ it also holds, and then both are zero. Thus **equality holds exactly when $p_2=p_3$**.

For the <discrete memoryless channel>, assume $\alpha,\beta\geq0$ and $\alpha+\beta\leq1$, and set $w=1-\alpha-\beta$. Both conditional output <probability distributions> have <Shannon entropy> $H(w,\alpha,\beta)$. The third output has <probability> $\beta$ regardless of the input; the first two have total <probability> $1-\beta$. Their <Shannon entropy> is maximized by making them equal, which a uniform input achieves. Hence the <channel capacity> is
$$
C=H\left(\frac{1-\beta}{2},\frac{1-\beta}{2},\beta\right)-H(w,\alpha,\beta)
$$
and therefore
$$
\boxed{C=(1-\beta)\bigl[1-\log_2(1-\beta)\bigr]+w\log_2w+\alpha\log_2\alpha.}
$$
The usual zero-times-log convention includes $\beta=1$, when $C=0$. If $w=\alpha$, the rows coincide and every input achieves zero <mutual information>; otherwise the uniform input is the unique maximizing input.

= 12E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= Solution
{parent=12E}

Choose a branch of $t=1-z$ with $|\arg t|<\pi$ and write $d=c-a-b\notin\mathbb Z$. The <Gauss hypergeometric equation> is
$$
z(1-z)y''+[c-(a+b+1)z]y'-aby=0.
$$
Changing to $t$ turns this into the same equation with third parameter $a+b+1-c=1-d$. Its solution regular at $t=0$ is $y_1=F(a,b,1-d;t)$. Substitution of $y=t^dv$ gives the <Gauss hypergeometric equation> for $v$ with parameters $c-a,c-b,1+d$. Thus $y_2=t^dF(c-a,c-b,1+d;t)$ is also a solution. Their distinct nonintegrally separated <characteristic exponents at a regular singular point> $0,d$ imply <linear independence> near $z=1$, so
$$
F(a,b,c;z)=Ay_1(z)+By_2(z).
$$
This also follows from the exponent transformation in the <Papperitz symbol>.

Initially assume $\Re c>\Re b>0$, so the <Euler integral for the hypergeometric function> applies. If $\Re d>0$, taking $z\to1$ gives an integral evaluated by the <beta function> and determines
$$
A=\frac{\Gamma(c)\Gamma(d)}{\Gamma(c-a)\Gamma(c-b)}.
$$
Indeed $y_1\to1$ and $y_2\to0$ there. To find $B$, instead work in the nonempty parameter region $\Re d<0$, put $z=1-\epsilon$ and scale the integration variable by $1-s=\epsilon u$. The singular contribution is
$$
\frac{\Gamma(c)}{\Gamma(b)\Gamma(c-b)}\epsilon^d
\int_0^\infty u^{c-b-1}(1+u)^{-a}\,du.
$$
The defining integral of the <beta function> evaluates this <integral> to $\Gamma(c-b)\Gamma(-d)/\Gamma(a)$. Since $\epsilon^{-d}y_1\to0$ and $\epsilon^{-d}y_2\to1$, it determines $B$. <Analytic continuation> in the parameters then yields the <hypergeometric connection formula>
$$
\boxed{A=\frac{\Gamma(c)\Gamma(c-a-b)}{\Gamma(c-a)\Gamma(c-b)},\qquad
B=\frac{\Gamma(c)\Gamma(a+b-c)}{\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)}.}
$$
The usual power-series definition requires $c\notin\{0,-1,-2,\ldots\}$; exceptional parameters need a separately specified analytic <limit>. Reciprocal <gamma functions> make vanishing coefficients at denominator poles meaningful. The branch must remain fixed when continuing in $z$.

= 13E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= Solution
{parent=13E}

Let $\mathbf r$ be position relative to the <center of mass>, and let the new origin have vector $\mathbf c$ relative to it. The <inertia tensor> about this origin is
$$
(I_{\mathbf c})_{ij}=\int\bigl(|\mathbf r-\mathbf c|^2\delta_{ij}-(r_i-c_i)(r_j-c_j)\bigr)\,dm.
$$
Expanding and using $\int\mathbf r\,dm=0$ proves the tensor form of the <parallel axis theorem>:
$$
\boxed{I_{\mathbf c}=I_0+M\bigl(|\mathbf c|^2I-\mathbf c\mathbf c^T\bigr).}
$$
For a uniform cube of side $L$ and mass $M$, centered coordinate <integrals> give $I_0=(ML^2/6)I$. The displacement to a vertex is $(L/2,L/2,L/2)$ up to signs. Using axes along its incident edges gives
$$
\boxed{I_{\rm vertex}=ML^2\begin{pmatrix}2/3&-1/4&-1/4\\-1/4&2/3&-1/4\\-1/4&-1/4&2/3\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The <principal moments of inertia> are $\boxed{ML^2/6,\ 11ML^2/12,\ 11ML^2/12}$. The first <principal axis> is the body diagonal through the vertex; every direction perpendicular to it has the repeated moment.

= 14H
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Logic and Set Theory}

= Solution
{parent=14h}

For <ordinals>, the inductive definition of <ordinal addition> is
$$
\alpha+0=\alpha,\quad\alpha+(\beta+1)=(\alpha+\beta)+1,\quad
\alpha+\lambda=\sup_{\beta<\lambda}(\alpha+\beta)\quad(\lambda\text{ a nonzero limit ordinal}).
$$
The synthetic definition takes a <well-order> of type $\alpha$, followed by a disjoint <well-order> of type $\beta$, with every point of the first preceding the second. They agree by <transfinite induction> on $\beta$: the empty second order does nothing; adding its last point takes the successor; at a <limit ordinal>, the concatenated order is the union of its initial concatenations, whose types have the displayed supremum.

= i
{parent=14h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The first assertion is **false**: $\boxed{1+\omega=\omega<\omega+1}$. Thus <ordinal addition> is not <commutative>.

= ii
{parent=14h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**False.** Put $\alpha=\beta=1$ and $\gamma=\omega$. Then
$$
\boxed{(\alpha+\beta)\gamma=2\omega=\omega,\qquad\alpha\gamma+\beta\gamma=\omega+\omega=\omega\,2.}
$$
The two <ordinals> differ. The order of the factors matters in the <distributive law for ordinal multiplication>.

= iii
{parent=14h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**True.** The valid <distributive law for ordinal multiplication> is
$$
\boxed{\alpha(\beta+\gamma)=\alpha\beta+\alpha\gamma.}
$$
The synthetic definition of <ordinal multiplication> takes a sequence of copies of the left factor, indexed by the right factor. A sequence indexed first by $\beta$ and then by $\gamma$ is exactly the concatenation of the two corresponding sequences. Taking <order types> gives the identity. Equivalently, use <transfinite induction> on $\gamma$, with the successor rule and the supremum rule at <limit ordinals>.

= iv
{parent=14h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**True.** If $\alpha\beta=\beta\alpha$, repeated use of <associativity> gives
$$
\boxed{\alpha^2\beta^2=\alpha(\alpha\beta)\beta=\alpha(\beta\alpha)\beta=(\alpha\beta)^2
=(\beta\alpha)^2=\beta^2\alpha^2.}
$$
No unlicensed <commutativity> of <ordinal multiplication> is being used: only the assumed commuting pair is swapped.

= v
{parent=14h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

**True.** A useful fact is that squaring is strictly increasing on <ordinals>. If $A<B$, monotonicity in both arguments gives $A^2\leq BA$, and strict increase in the right argument for the positive left factor $B$ gives $BA<B^2$. Hence $A^2<B^2$.

Suppose, for a contradiction, that $\alpha\beta<\beta\alpha$. Again using monotonicity and <associativity> of <ordinal multiplication>,
$$
\alpha^2\beta^2=\alpha(\alpha\beta)\beta
\leq\alpha(\beta\alpha)\beta=(\alpha\beta)^2
<(\beta\alpha)^2=\beta(\alpha\beta)\alpha
\leq\beta(\beta\alpha)\alpha=\beta^2\alpha^2.
$$
This contradicts the hypothesized equality of the outer terms. Reversing the roles excludes $\beta\alpha<\alpha\beta$. If either factor is zero, they already commute. Therefore the <commuting squares of ordinals> property is
$$
\boxed{\alpha^2\beta^2=\beta^2\alpha^2\ \Longrightarrow\ \alpha\beta=\beta\alpha.}
$$
The proof uses monotonicity, which follows directly from the ordered-copy definition; it does not assume a cancellation law for <ordinals>.

= 15H
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Graph Theory}

= Solution
{parent=15H}

For a finite <bipartite graph> with left vertex set $L$, <Hall marriage theorem> says that a <matching in a graph> covering $L$ exists exactly when $|N(S)|\geq|S|$ for every $S\subseteq L$. Necessity follows because distinct matched neighbors of $S$ lie in $N(S)$.

For sufficiency, induct on $|L|$, the empty case being immediate. If a nonempty proper subset $S$ has $|N(S)|=|S|$, apply the induction hypothesis to the <graph> on $S,N(S)$. For the remaining <graph>, every $T\subseteq L\setminus S$ satisfies
$$
|N(T)\setminus N(S)|=|N(T\cup S)|-|N(S)|\geq|T|,
$$
so induction matches the remaining vertices too. If there is no such tight subset and $|L|>1$, every nonempty proper subset has at least one extra neighbor. Select an edge and remove its endpoints. Each subset of the remaining left vertices loses at most one neighbor, so it still satisfies the required condition; induction supplies the rest of the <matching in a graph>. The case $|L|=1$ follows directly from the condition.

For a <doubly stochastic matrix> $A=(a_{ij})$, make a <bipartite graph> joining row $i$ to column $j$ when $a_{ij}>0$. For a row subset $S$,
$$
|S|=\sum_{i\in S}\sum_{j\in N(S)}a_{ij}
\leq\sum_{j\in N(S)}\sum_i a_{ij}=|N(S)|.
$$
<Hall marriage theorem> therefore gives a <perfect matching>, or a <permutation> $\sigma$, with $\boxed{a_{i,\sigma(i)}>0\text{ for every }i}$.

= 16I
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Galois Theory}

= a
{parent=16i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A finite <field extension> $L/K$ is a <separable field extension> if every element of $L$ has a <minimal polynomial> over $K$ with distinct roots in an <algebraic closure>. The <primitive element theorem> says that a finite <separable field extension> $L/K$ is $K(\alpha)$ for some $\alpha\in L$.

If $K$ is finite, then so is $L$. The multiplicative group of a <finite field> is cyclic, so a generator of $L^\times$ generates $L$ as a field. To justify the group fact, a finite subgroup of a field's multiplicative group is cyclic: in a <finite abelian group> its exponent is attained as an element order, while the <polynomial> root bound forces its size to be at most that exponent.

If $K$ is infinite, first consider $L=K(a,b)$. The <minimal polynomials> of $a$ and $b$ have distinct roots $a_i,b_j$ in an <algebraic closure>. Choose $c\in K$, $c\ne0$, avoiding the finitely many values for which $a_i+cb_j=a+cb$ for a pair other than $(a,b)$. Set $\alpha=a+cb$. Over $K(\alpha)$, the <polynomials> $f(T)$ and $g((\alpha-T)/c)$ have precisely one common root, $a$. Their monic <greatest common divisor> is therefore $T-a$, so $a\in K(\alpha)$ and $b=(\alpha-a)/c\in K(\alpha)$. Thus $K(a,b)=K(\alpha)$. Induct over finitely many generators of the finite <separable field extension>. This proves **every finite separable extension is simple**.

= b
{parent=16i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $a=\sqrt p$, $b=\sqrt{-q}$ and $\alpha=a+b$. Since $a^2-b^2=p+q$, the identity $(a+b)(a-b)=p+q$ gives
$$
a=\frac12\left(\alpha+\frac{p+q}{\alpha}\right),\qquad
b=\frac12\left(\alpha-\frac{p+q}{\alpha}\right).
$$
Thus $\mathbb Q(\alpha)=\mathbb Q(\sqrt p,\sqrt{-q})$. The latter has degree $4$: $p$ is not a square and $\mathbb Q(\sqrt p)$ is real, whereas $\sqrt{-q}$ is not real. Squaring $\alpha^2=p-q+2\sqrt{-pq}$ gives its monic degree-$4$ <minimal polynomial>
$$
\boxed{m_\alpha(T)=T^4-2(p-q)T^2+(p+q)^2.}
$$

= c
{parent=16i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

In characteristic $p$, let $K=\mathbb F_p(s,t)$ and $L=\mathbb F_p(s^{1/p},t^{1/p})$, with $s,t$ having <algebraic independence>. The monomials $s^{i/p}t^{j/p}$, $0\leq i,j<p$, form a $K$-basis, so $[L:K]=p^2$. Their independence follows by grouping <polynomial> monomials according to their exponents modulo $p$, after clearing rational-function denominators.

Every $\alpha\in L$ has $\alpha^p\in K$, so its <minimal polynomial> divides $T^p-\alpha^p$ and $[K(\alpha):K]\leq p$. Thus **this finite purely inseparable extension has no primitive element**. It demonstrates why the <separable field extension> hypothesis cannot simply be removed.

= 17G
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Representation Theory}

= a
{parent=17g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $g$ have order $n$ in a finite group. In a complex <group representation>, its <matrix> is diagonalizable with <eigenvalues> $\zeta_j$ satisfying $\zeta_j^n=1$. If each $g^i$ with $\gcd(i,n)=1$ is conjugate to $g$, the <character of a representation> is constant on these powers. Hence
$$
\varphi(n)\chi(g)=\sum_j\sum_{\substack{1\leq i\leq n\\(i,n)=1}}\zeta_j^i\in\mathbb Z,
$$
using the stated integrality of each <Ramanujan sum>. Therefore $\chi(g)$ is rational. It is also an <algebraic integer>, being a sum of <roots of unity>; a rational <algebraic integer> is an <integer>. Thus $\boxed{\chi(g)\in\mathbb Z}$.

In a <symmetric group>, raising a <permutation> to a power coprime to its order preserves each cycle length and therefore its entire cycle type. Since cycle type determines the <conjugacy class>, the hypothesis holds, and **all character values of <symmetric groups> are <integers>**.

= b
{parent=17g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Suppose $\chi$ is an <irreducible character> of a group of odd order and $\chi=\overline\chi$. Write $d=\chi(1)$. Every nonidentity element pairs with its distinct inverse, since there is no element of order $2$. Also $\chi(g^{-1})=\overline{\chi(g)}=\chi(g)$. If $\chi$ were nontrivial, <character orthogonality> with the trivial <character of a representation> would give
$$
\langle\chi,1_G\rangle=\frac{d+2A}{|G|},\qquad 0=d+2A,
$$
where $A$ is the sum of one <character of a representation> value from each inverse pair. This $A$ is an <algebraic integer>, so $A=-d/2\in\mathbb Q$ is an <integer>. Hence $d$ is even.

But an <irreducible character degree divides the group order>, so $d$ must be odd. One quick justification of that divisibility is that the central class sum of a <conjugacy class> $C$ acts by the <algebraic integer> $\lambda_C=|C|\chi(g_C)/d$. Its integrality follows from its <integer> <matrix> on the regular <group representation>. <Character orthogonality> then gives
$$
\frac{|G|}{d}=\sum_C\lambda_C\overline{\chi(g_C)},
$$
a rational <algebraic integer>, hence an <integer>. The parity contradiction proves $\boxed{\chi=1_G}$: **the trivial character is the only self-conjugate <irreducible character> of an odd-order group**.

= 18H
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Number Fields}

= a
{parent=18h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a <number field> with $r$ real embeddings $\sigma_i$ and $s$ conjugate pairs of complex embeddings $\tau_j$, use the <logarithmic embedding of number field units>
$$
\ell(u)=(\log|\sigma_1u|,\ldots,\log|\sigma_ru|,2\log|\tau_1u|,\ldots,2\log|\tau_su|).
$$
It is a <group homomorphism> from multiplication to addition. A <unit> has <field norm> $\pm1$, so the coordinates sum to zero. Deleting one coordinate identifies this hyperplane with $\mathbb R^{r+s-1}$ and gives the desired map.

If its image coordinates remain in a bounded set, the omitted coordinate is bounded too, and every conjugate of $u$ has bounded absolute value. The coefficients of its monic <minimal polynomial> are <integers> and elementary symmetric expressions in at most $[K:\mathbb Q]$ bounded conjugates. They are therefore bounded <integers>. Only finitely many such <polynomials>, and finitely many roots of them, exist. Thus the inverse image of every bounded set is finite. In particular **the kernel is finite and the image is discrete**. This argument includes the zero-dimensional target case; it does not yet assert the full-rank part of <Dirichlet unit theorem>.

= b
{parent=18h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the real quadratic field $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt d)$ with squarefree $d>1$, <Dirichlet unit theorem> gives
$$
\boxed{\mathcal O_K^\times=\{\pm\epsilon^n:n\in\mathbb Z\}}
$$
for a <fundamental unit> $\epsilon>1$, since the <roots of unity> in a real field are $\pm1$.

If $p\mid d$ and $p\equiv3\pmod4$, a <unit> of norm $-1$ would have the form $(a+b\sqrt d)/2$ with <integers> $a,b$ and satisfy $a^2-db^2=-4$. Reducing modulo the odd <prime> $p$ gives $(a/2)^2\equiv-1\pmod p$, impossible because $-1$ is not a <quadratic residue> for such a <prime>. Hence **every <unit> has norm $+1$ in this case**. In contrast, $\boxed{d=2,\ u=1+\sqrt2}$ has norm $-1$.

= 19I
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Topology}

= a
{parent=19i}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <pushout of groups> is
$$
G=(G_1*G_2)/\left\langle\!\left\langle i_1(h)i_2(h)^{-1}:h\in H\right\rangle\!\right\rangle.
$$
Its maps from $G_1,G_2$ agree on $H$, and every other pair of <group homomorphisms> agreeing on $H$ factors through one unique homomorphism from $G$. This is the <universal property>. When the $i_j$ are injective, it is the <amalgamated free product>, and the two factors and the identified copy of $H$ embed in it. Some conventions use this name for the quotient even without injectivity; the embedding assertion requires injectivity.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <Seifert-van Kampen theorem> states: if $X=U\cup V$, where $U,V$ are open and path connected, $U\cap V$ is path connected and contains a basepoint $x$, then $\pi_1(X,x)$ is the <pushout of groups> of $\pi_1(U,x)$ and $\pi_1(V,x)$ over $\pi_1(U\cap V,x)$. Thus **the <fundamental group> is obtained by amalgamating the overlap relations**. Openness can be replaced by standard neighborhood hypotheses, as in the wedge calculation.

= b
{parent=19i}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <real projective plane> has <fundamental group> $C_2=\langle a\mid a^2=1\rangle$, while the <circle> has <fundamental group> $\mathbb Z=\langle b\rangle$. Apply the <Seifert-van Kampen theorem> to open neighborhoods of the two wedge summands whose overlap is contractible. This gives
$$
\boxed{\pi_1(\mathbb {RP}^2\vee S^1,x)\cong C_2*\mathbb Z=\langle a,b\mid a^2=1\rangle.}
$$
Changing the basepoint within this path-connected space gives an isomorphic group.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Define $\boxed{\phi(a)=(12),\quad\phi(b)=(123)}$. The relation $a^2=1$ is respected, so the <group presentation> gives a homomorphism to $S_3$. These <permutations> generate $S_3$: conjugating $(12)$ by powers of $(123)$ produces the other transpositions. Hence **$\phi$ is surjective**.

= c
{parent=19i}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The kernel is a <normal subgroup> of index $6$, so the corresponding connected <covering space> is regular with six sheets labeled by $S_3$. Write $a=(12)$, $b=(123)$ and use right multiplication for path lifting. Above the <real projective plane>, right multiplication by $a$ pairs the labels, giving three copies of its two-sheeted <covering space> $S^2$. Each <sphere> has two marked antipodal points corresponding to its pair $\{b^i,b^ia\}$, $i=0,1,2$. Above the <circle>, right multiplication by $b$ gives two cycles of length three. Attach their six marked points to the identically labeled points of these three <spheres>.

\Image[paper-2-cover.png]
{title=The six-sheeted cover: three spheres joined by two lifted circles}

The upper <circle> follows $e\to b\to b^2\to e$; the lower follows $a\to ab\to ab^2\to a$. The point paired with $b^i$ is $b^ia=ab^{-i}$, explaining the reversed lower labeling in the figure. On each summand these are genuine <covering maps>, and the attachment matches the six lifts of the wedge point. A small neighborhood of that point lifts to six disjoint copies of its neighborhood in the wedge. This verifies the <covering space> locally, and the action of $a,b$ is transitive, so it is connected. Its stabilizer is $\ker\phi$, identifying this <kernel cover of a real projective plane wedged with a circle>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**Yes, it retracts onto a <graph>.** On each of the three <spheres>, choose an arc joining the two marked points. A <sphere> retracts onto this arc: identify it with the vertical meridian parameterized by the height coordinate, and send every point of a given height to the meridian point at that height. This map is <continuous> and fixes the meridian. Apply these three <retractions> and fix the lifted <circles> pointwise; the maps agree at all attachment points. The resulting connected <graph> has six vertices and nine edges, so its <fundamental group> is free of rank $\boxed{9-6+1=4}$.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**No, it cannot deformation retract onto a <graph>.** Each <sphere> contributes a degree-$2$ <homology> class independent of the other two. Attaching <circles> at points introduces only vertices and edges, so
$$
\boxed{H_2(\widehat X;\mathbb Z)\cong\mathbb Z^3.}
$$
For example, the <cellular chain complex> obtained by giving each <sphere> one $2$-cell and a <tree> of arcs has three zero-boundary $2$-cells and no $3$-cells. Every <graph> has zero degree-$2$ <homology>. A <deformation retraction> would induce a <homotopy equivalence> and an isomorphism on <homology>, which is impossible. An ordinary <retraction> has no such requirement.

= 20F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Analysis}

= a
{parent=20f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Because $Y$ is closed, it is a <nowhere dense set> exactly when its interior is empty. If a ball about $y\in Y$ lay inside $Y$, translating it by $-y$ would put a ball about $0$ inside $Y$. Every vector of $X$ has a small scalar multiple in that ball, and closure under <scalar multiplication> would force $Y=X$. This contradiction proves **a proper closed <linear subspace> is nowhere dense**.

= b
{parent=20f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

One form of the <Baire category theorem> is: **a nonempty <complete metric space> cannot be a countable union of closed nowhere dense subsets**. Equivalently, a countable intersection of open dense subsets is dense. A <Banach space> is a <complete metric space> for its norm metric, so this form applies directly to the next part.

= c
{parent=20f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Suppose $(x_k)_{k\geq1}$ were a countable <Hamel basis>. Put $E_n=\operatorname{span}(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$. Each $E_n$ is a finite-dimensional, hence closed, <linear subspace> of $X$. It is proper because $X$ is infinite dimensional, and therefore a <nowhere dense set>. A <Hamel basis> means that every vector is a finite <linear combination>, so $X=\bigcup_nE_n$. This contradicts the <Baire category theorem> for the <Banach space> $X$. Hence **an infinite-dimensional Banach space has no countable Hamel basis**. The same proof excludes any countable algebraic spanning set.

= 21F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Riemann Surfaces}

= Solution
{parent=21F}

By the <argument principle>, the number of zeros minus poles, counted with multiplicity, in the fundamental parallelogram is $(2\pi i)^{-1}\oint f'/f\,dz$. The logarithmic <derivative> is periodic with the same lattice, so the <integrals> over opposite sides cancel. Thus **the numbers of zeros and poles are equal**.

The <Weierstrass elliptic function> gives a degree-two map $\wp:\mathbb C/\Lambda\to\mathbb P^1$ whose fibers identify $z$ and $-z$. An even <elliptic function> is constant on these fibers, so it descends to a single-valued function $Q$ on the <sphere>. This is meromorphic even at a branch value: near a half-period the involution is $t\mapsto-t$, the <Laurent series> of $f$ has only even powers, and $\wp-\wp_0$ has a double zero and provides a local coordinate in $t^2$. At the image of $0$, $\wp\sim t^{-2}$ gives the same conclusion at infinity. A <meromorphic function> on the <Riemann sphere> is a <rational function>, so $\boxed{f=Q(\wp)}$.

**A meromorphic antiderivative need not be elliptic.** Take $f=\wp$. Its double poles have zero <residue>, so integrating on the punctured plane gives a single-valued primitive, since every closed-contour <integral> is a sum of zero <residues>. The primitive extends meromorphically over each pole, with local principal part $-1/z$. If $F$ were elliptic, it would have precisely one pole modulo the lattice, with <residue> $-1$, contradicting the zero sum of <residues> in a fundamental parallelogram. This is a nonconstant counterexample satisfying the stated premise; the preceding descent proves that <even elliptic functions are rational in the Weierstrass function>.

= 22I
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Geometry}

= a
{parent=22i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A point $P$ belongs to the <smooth locus of a variety> if its <local ring> $\mathcal O_{X,P}$ is a <regular local ring>, meaning
$$
\boxed{\dim_k(\mathfrak m_P/\mathfrak m_P^2)=\dim\mathcal O_{X,P}.}
$$
Equivalently, the <Zariski tangent space> has the expected local dimension. Over the algebraically closed field, this agrees with smoothness for a reduced variety. In affine coordinates, the <Jacobian criterion> expresses it by the rank of the <matrix> of first <derivatives> of defining equations.

= b
{parent=22i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Jacobian criterion> on an affine chart $x_j\ne0$ says that a point of the <projective hypersurface> is singular exactly when all <derivatives> of its dehomogenized equation vanish there. The <Euler homogeneous function theorem> identity $\sum_ix_if_{x_i}=df$ then recovers the omitted $j$th <derivative> on $X$, without dividing by $d$. Thus the correct statement in every characteristic is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Sing}(X)=X\cap V(f_{x_0},\ldots,f_{x_n}).}
$$
If the printed $Z(I)$ denotes a zero locus inside $X$, this proves it as written. If it means the ambient projective zero locus, the assertion is false when the characteristic divides $d$. When the characteristic does not divide $d$, the <Euler homogeneous function theorem> forces $f=0$ at every common zero of the <derivatives>, and the intersection with $X$ is redundant.

For a genuine counterexample to the unrestricted ambient interpretation, in characteristic $p$ take
$$
f=X_0^p+X_1^{p-1}X_2.
$$
This is an irreducible <homogeneous polynomial>: it is primitive and linear in $X_2$ over $k[X_0,X_1]$, with coprime coefficients. At $[1:0:0]$ all <partial derivatives> vanish, including when $p=2$, but $f=1$. This point is outside $X$. Hence **the ambient-locus version needs the displayed intersection or a restriction on the degree**.

= c
{parent=22i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Homogenization gives $F=X^4+X^2Y^2+Y^2Z^2+Z^4$, with
$$
F_X=4X^3+2XY^2,\qquad F_Y=2Y(X^2+Z^2),\qquad F_Z=2ZY^2+4Z^3.
$$
If the characteristic is not $2$, a singular point with $Y=0$ would force $X=Z=0$, impossible. If $Y\ne0$, then $X^2+Z^2=0$. If either $X$ or $Z$ vanishes, both vanish, giving $[0:1:0]$. Otherwise the other two <derivative> equations require $Y^2=-2X^2=-2Z^2$, whence $X^2=Z^2$ and $2X^2=0$, again impossible. Thus
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Sing}(X)=\{[0:1:0]\}\quad(\operatorname{char}k\ne2).}
$$
In the chart $Y=1$, the quadratic term is $X^2+Z^2$, the product of two distinct linear factors over $k$, so this is an <ordinary double point>.

In characteristic $2$,
$$
\boxed{F=(X^2+XY+YZ+Z^2)^2=((X+Z)(X+Y+Z))^2.}
$$
All first <derivatives> of this equation vanish. The equation defines a <nonreduced scheme>, singular everywhere; it no longer satisfies the irreducible/reduced hypersurface premise. If “curve” means the associated reduced variety, it is the union of two distinct lines, and only their intersection $\boxed{[1:0:1]}$ is singular. These two interpretations must not be conflated.

= 23I
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Geometry}

= Solution
{parent=23I}

For a regular parameter $t$, the <curvature> and <torsion of a space curve> are
$$
\kappa=\frac{|\alpha'\times\alpha''|}{|\alpha'|^3},\qquad
\tau=\frac{\det(\alpha',\alpha'',\alpha''')}{|\alpha'\times\alpha''|^2}.
$$
The <torsion of a space curve> requires $\kappa\ne0$. Reparameterize by <arc length> $s$ and set $T=\alpha_s$, $N=T_s/\kappa$, $B=T\times N$. Differentiating the pairwise inner products of this orthonormal <Frenet frame> shows that its <derivative> <matrix> is skew symmetric. Since $T_s=\kappa N$, defining $\tau=N_s\cdot B$ gives the <Frenet-Serret formulas>
$$
\boxed{T_s=\kappa N,\quad N_s=-\kappa T+\tau B,\quad B_s=-\tau N.}
$$
The <fundamental theorem of regular space curves> says that prescribed smooth $\kappa>0$ and smooth $\tau$ on an interval determine a unit-speed curve uniquely up to an orientation-preserving Euclidean rigid motion. Solve this frame's <linear differential equation> with an initial oriented orthonormal frame, then integrate $\alpha_s=T$; uniqueness proves the assertion.

Under the intended convention that the shared parameter $s$ is <arc length>, equality on an open interval gives equal position and equal <Frenet frames> at one point. Equal $\kappa,\tau$ give identical differential equations, so uniqueness gives $\boxed{\widetilde\alpha=\alpha\text{ on }I}$. The printed “regular smooth” hypothesis alone does not establish this convention. Without common <arc length>, an orientation-preserving smooth change of speed that is the identity on $J$ but not elsewhere reparameterizes a <circle> into a counterexample: both curves have $\kappa=1$, $\tau=0$ and agree on $J$, but differ elsewhere as parameterized curves.

For an oriented surface with <unit normal> $n$, define <normal curvature> $\kappa_n=T_s\cdot n$ and <geodesic curvature> $\kappa_g=T_s\cdot(n\times T)$. Thus
$$
T_s=\kappa_g(n\times T)+\kappa_n n.
$$
A unit-speed curve is a <geodesic> exactly when $\kappa_g=0$. If also $\kappa_n\equiv0$, then $T_s=0$, so it is a straight line. A vertical ruling of a circular cylinder is such a <geodesic>, although the cylinder contains no open piece of a plane. A nonconstant regular straight line cannot be a simple closed curve. Hence **no planar patch is required, and such a <geodesic> cannot be simple and closed**.

Finally let the <principal curvatures> be $\kappa_1,\kappa_2$. If the <Gaussian curvature> $K=\kappa_1\kappa_2\geq0$, they have the same sign or one vanishes. <Euler formula for normal curvature> makes $\kappa_n$ a weighted average of them. For a <geodesic>, $\kappa=|\kappa_n|$, so
$$
\boxed{\kappa\leq\max(|\kappa_1|,|\kappa_2|)\leq|\kappa_1+\kappa_2|=2|H|.}
$$
A horizontal <circle> on a cylinder of radius $R$ is a <geodesic> with $\kappa=1/R$, $K=0$, $|H|=1/(2R)$, attaining equality.

= 24J
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Probability and Measure}

= a
{parent=24j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $f\in L^1(\mathbb R^d)$, use the <Fourier transform> convention
$$
\boxed{\widehat f(\xi)=\int_{\mathbb R^d}e^{-i\xi\cdot x}f(x)\,dx.}
$$
The <integral> is absolutely convergent for every $\xi$, and $|\widehat f(\xi)|\leq\|f\|_1$.

= b
{parent=24j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Here <Fourier inversion> means the absolute-integral version: $\widehat f\in L^1(\mathbb R^d)$ and
$$
\boxed{f(x)=(2\pi)^{-d}\int_{\mathbb R^d}e^{i\xi\cdot x}\widehat f(\xi)\,d\xi\quad\text{for almost every }x.}
$$
This convention is necessary for the bounded <continuous> version in part (d). Conditional inversion by symmetric truncation is a different statement and need not have that conclusion.

= c
{parent=24j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

First $\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-x^2/2}\,dx=\sqrt{2\pi}$: square the positive <integral> and use polar coordinates to obtain $2\pi\int_0^\infty re^{-r^2/2}\,dr=2\pi$. Scaling therefore normalizes the one-dimensional <Gaussian function> $g_t$.

Let $I(\xi)=\int_{\mathbb R}(2\pi t)^{-1/2}e^{-x^2/(2t)}e^{-i\xi x}\,dx$. Differentiation under the <integral> is justified by the integrable majorant $|x|g_t(x)$. Since $g_t'(x)=-xg_t(x)/t$, <integration by parts>, with zero endpoint terms, gives $I'(\xi)=-t\xi I(\xi)$. With $I(0)=1$, this yields $I(\xi)=e^{-t\xi^2/2}$. Multiplication of the one-dimensional <integrals> gives
$$
\boxed{\widehat g_t(\xi)=e^{-t|\xi|^2/2}.}
$$
Applying the same computation with reciprocal scale $1/t$ evaluates its inverse <integral>:
$$
(2\pi)^{-d}\int e^{i\xi\cdot x}e^{-t|\xi|^2/2}\,d\xi
=(2\pi t)^{-d/2}e^{-|x|^2/(2t)}=g_t(x).
$$
For $f\in L^1$, <Fubini's theorem> gives $\widehat{f*g_t}=\widehat f\,e^{-t|\xi|^2/2}$, which is integrable because $\widehat f$ is bounded. Its inverse <integral> is
$$
\int_{\mathbb R^d} f(y)\left[(2\pi)^{-d}\int_{\mathbb R^d}e^{i\xi\cdot(x-y)}e^{-t|\xi|^2/2}\,d\xi\right]dy
=\int f(y)g_t(x-y)\,dy.
$$
Absolute integrability of $|f(y)|e^{-t|\xi|^2/2}$ justifies this second application of <Fubini's theorem>. Thus **<Fourier inversion> holds for every Gaussian <convolution>**, in fact pointwise for this <continuous> representative.

= d
{parent=24j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Define $g$ by the inverse <integral>. Its absolute convergence gives $|g(x)|\leq(2\pi)^{-d}\|\widehat f\|_1$. If $x_n\to x$, the exponential factors converge pointwise and their differences are bounded by $2|\widehat f|$. The <dominated convergence theorem> proves $g(x_n)\to g(x)$. Therefore **$g$ is bounded and <continuous> and equals $f$ almost everywhere**.

= e
{parent=24j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

**No, under the absolute-integral convention.** Direct integration gives
$$
\widehat {\mathbf1_{[0,1]}}(\xi)=\frac{1-e^{-i\xi}}{i\xi},\qquad
\left|\widehat {\mathbf1_{[0,1]}}(\xi)\right|=\frac{2|\sin(\xi/2)|}{|\xi|}.
$$
On a fixed positive-length portion of every period, the numerator is bounded below. Summing the resulting lower bounds gives a divergent harmonic series, so the <Fourier transform> is not in $L^1$. Also there is no <continuous> almost-everywhere representative: <continuity> would force it to be $1$ on $(0,1)$ and $0$ on $(-\infty,0)$, giving incompatible <limits> at $0$. Symmetrically truncated conditional inversion does hold at interior <continuity> points and gives half the jump value at the endpoints; this does not contradict the answer.

= 25K
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Applied Probability}

= a
{parent=25k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A rate-$\lambda$ <Poisson process> has $N_0=0$, right-continuous paths with upward jumps of size one, stationary <independent> increments, and $N_t-N_s\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda(t-s))$ for $t\geq s$. Assume $\lambda>0$. Its successive waiting times are <independent> exponential variables of rate $\lambda$. For $0<t_1<\cdots<t_n<t$, the product of their densities and the <probability> of no further jump before $t$ is
$$
\lambda^ne^{-\lambda t_1}e^{-\lambda(t_2-t_1)}\cdots e^{-\lambda(t_n-t_{n-1})}e^{-\lambda(t-t_n)}=\lambda^ne^{-\lambda t}.
$$
Divide by $\mathbb P(N_t=n)=e^{-\lambda t}(\lambda t)^n/n!$ to obtain the conditional joint density
$$
\boxed{\frac{n!}{t^n}\mathbf1_{\{0<t_1<\cdots<t_n<t\}}.}
$$
Boundary inequalities do not affect a density. Equivalently these are the ordered values of $n$ <independent> uniform points of $[0,t]$. For $n=0$, the conditional tuple is empty.

= b
{parent=25k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Assume the zero set $A=\{s\in[0,t]:g(s)=0\}$ is measurable, as is ensured by measurability of $g$. This is an implicit necessary hypothesis: for an arbitrary real function the printed <integral> and event need not be measurable. Put $a=\int_0^t\mathbf1_A(s)\,ds$. Conditional on $N_t=n$, the <uniform order statistics> description makes the <probability> of no jump in $A$ equal to $(1-a/t)^n$. Averaging over the <Poisson distribution> gives
$$
\mathbb P(0\notin\mathcal R(g)[0,t])
=e^{-\lambda t}\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{[\lambda t(1-a/t)]^n}{n!}=e^{-\lambda a}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(0\in\mathcal R(g)[0,t])=1-\exp\left(-\lambda\int_0^t\mathbf1_{\{g(s)=0\}}\,ds\right).}
$$
In particular a measure-zero zero set is almost surely missed, despite possibly containing infinitely many points.

= 26K
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Statistics}

= a
{parent=26k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Put $r=1-\alpha\in(0,1)$. The variable $Y=X_1-\theta$ has density $ry^{r-1}$ on $(0,1)$, so $\mathbb EY^j=r/(r+j)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbb EX_1=\theta+\frac r{r+1},\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(X_1)=\frac r{(r+2)(r+1)^2}.}
$$
The <unbiased estimator> is
$$
\boxed{\widetilde\theta_n=\overline X_n-\frac{1-\alpha}{2-\alpha},\qquad c(\alpha)=-\frac{1-\alpha}{2-\alpha}.}
$$
Taking <expectations> verifies that its <bias> is zero.

= b
{parent=26k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The observations have finite mean and <variance>. The <strong law of large numbers> gives $\overline X_n\to\mathbb EX_1$ almost surely, so $\widetilde\theta_n\to\theta$ almost surely and therefore is a <consistent estimator>. The <central limit theorem> gives
$$
\boxed{\sqrt n(\widetilde\theta_n-\theta)\ \xrightarrow{d}\ N\left(0,\frac{1-\alpha}{(3-\alpha)(2-\alpha)^2}\right).}
$$

= c
{parent=26k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The support restricts $\theta$ to $\theta>0$, $\theta\leq X_{(1)}$ and $\theta\geq X_{(n)}-1$. Within its feasible interior, the <likelihood function> is proportional to $\prod_i(X_i-\theta)^{-\alpha}$ and is strictly increasing in $\theta$, diverging as $\theta\uparrow X_{(1)}$. Under the printed infinite-endpoint-density, or extended-likelihood convention, the <maximum likelihood estimator> is
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta_n=X_{(1)}.}
$$
A density is defined only almost everywhere. If its value at the singular endpoint is reassigned a finite value, a finite maximum need not exist; the sample-minimum answer uses the stated endpoint/supremum convention.

Independence and $\mathbb P(Y\leq t)=t^r$ on $[0,1]$ give
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(\widehat\theta_n-\theta>t)=\begin{cases}1,&t\leq0,\\(1-t^r)^n,&0<t<1,\\0,&t\geq1.\end{cases}}
$$
The error is positive almost surely, so the estimator is not an <unbiased estimator>. More explicitly its <bias> is
$$
\mathbb E(\widehat\theta_n-\theta)=\int_0^1(1-t^r)^n\,dt
=\frac{\Gamma(1+1/r)\Gamma(n+1)}{\Gamma(n+1+1/r)}>0.
$$

= d
{parent=26k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Set $\boxed{\beta=1/(1-\alpha)=1/r}$. For fixed $t>0$ and large $n$,
$$
\mathbb P\bigl(n^{1/r}(\widehat\theta_n-\theta)>t\bigr)
=\left(1-\frac{t^r}{n}\right)^n\longrightarrow\boxed{e^{-t^r}}.
$$
This <endpoint maximum likelihood for a singular location density> calculation gives a <Weibull distribution> with shape $r$. The estimator is a <consistent estimator>, and its error has order $n^{-1/r}$, faster than the $n^{-1/2}$ error of $\widetilde\theta_n$. Its <bias> is asymptotic to $\Gamma(1+1/r)n^{-1/r}$ and its <mean squared error> to $\Gamma(1+2/r)n^{-2/r}$, versus a positive constant times $n^{-1}$ for the sample-mean estimator. Thus **the minimum estimator is preferable for its much faster convergence**, despite its positive finite-sample <bias>. This is a nonregular support-dependent model; ordinary regular-model efficiency bounds do not apply.

= 27J
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Stochastic Financial Models}

= a
{parent=27j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A standard <Brownian motion> is a real process $(B_t)_{t\geq0}$ with $B_0=0$ almost surely, <continuous> paths, <independent> increments, and $B_t-B_s\sim N(0,t-s)$ for $0\leq s<t$. If a filtration is specified, require adaptedness and independence of each increment from the past sigma-algebra. Thus **the increments are centered Gaussian with <variance> equal to elapsed time**.

= b
{parent=27j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The scaled process starts at zero and has <continuous> paths. Disjoint time intervals map to disjoint intervals under $t\mapsto c^2t$, preserving independence of increments, and
$$
\frac{B_{c^2t}-B_{c^2s}}c\sim N\left(0,\frac{c^2(t-s)}{c^2}\right)=N(0,t-s).
$$
This holds for either sign of $c\ne0$. Hence $\boxed{\widetilde B_t=c^{-1}B_{c^2t}\text{ is standard Brownian motion}}$.

= c
{parent=27j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $c>0$, <Brownian scaling> and the bijection $t\mapsto c^2t$ of $[0,\infty)$ give $Z\overset d=c^{-1}Z$, or $\boxed{cZ\overset d=Z}$. The supremum is a measurable extended <random variable> because <continuity> allows the supremum over rational times, and $Z\geq0$.

For $z>0$, the function $F(z)=\mathbb P(Z\leq z)$ must satisfy $F(z)=F(z/c)$ for every $c>0$, so it is constant on $(0,\infty)$. Letting $z\downarrow0$ and $z\uparrow\infty$ shows $\mathbb P(Z=0)=\mathbb P(Z<\infty)$, hence $\boxed{Z\in\{0,+\infty\}\text{ almost surely}}$. This scaling argument alone does not decide the mass at zero; part (d) does.

= d
{parent=27j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For $a>0$, the <Brownian reflection principle> gives $\mathbb P(\sup_{0\leq s\leq T}B_s\geq a)=2\mathbb P(B_T\geq a)$. Reflection of the path after its first hit of $a$ preserves its law and pairs paths ending below $a$ with paths ending above $a$, proving this identity. Therefore
$$
\mathbb P\left(\sup_{0\leq s\leq T}B_s\leq a\right)=2\Phi(a/\sqrt T)-1\longrightarrow0.
$$
By monotone increase of the finite-time suprema, the <probability> that the full supremum is at most any finite $a>0$ is zero. Taking a countable union over <integer> $a$ gives $\boxed{\mathbb P(Z=+\infty)=1}$.

= 28K
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Optimization and Control}

= a
{parent=28k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Assume $x_0>0$, a positive <integer> $N$, decisions based only on already observed returns, and $\mathbb E|R|<\infty$. The last is a sufficient well-posedness condition absent from the print; without moment assumptions the objectives may be infinite. Put $r=1/N$. Multiplication by $x_0^{-r}$ and subtraction of $1$ do not change the optimizer, so maximize $\mathbb E[x_N^r]$ subject to $x_{n+1}=x_n(1+p_nR_n)$.

The <Bellman equation> for the <value function> is
$$
\boxed{J_N(x)=x^r,\qquad J_n(x)=\max_{0\leq p\leq1}\mathbb E[J_{n+1}(x(1+pR))].}
$$
The <expectation> uses a fresh <independent> return, since the action is chosen before it is observed. Zero wealth remains zero.

= b
{parent=28k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $M=\max_{p\in[0,1]}\mathbb E(1+pR)^r$ and choose an optimizer $p^*$. Existence follows from <continuity> and <compactness>: the integrands are dominated by a constant times $1+|R|$. Induction in the <Bellman equation> gives
$$
\boxed{J_n(x)=x^rM^{N-n},\qquad p_n=p^*\text{ is optimal at every period}.}
$$
Thus the maximal expected yield is $\boxed{M^N-1}$. Neither current wealth nor time changes the maximizing fraction; there may be several optimizers.

The right <derivative> at $p=0$ is $r\mathbb ER$, using the finite first moment and dominated differentiation near zero. If $\mathbb ER>0$, increasing $p$ slightly improves on $p=0$, so $\boxed{p^*>0}$. No explicit value of $p^*$ is needed.

= c
{parent=28k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For expected final capital, the <Bellman equation> is linear in wealth, with one-period factor $\mathbb E(1+pR)=1+p\mathbb ER$. Therefore **invest everything when the mean return is positive, nothing when it is negative, and any fraction when it is zero**. For $N=1$ this agrees with the yield objective.

For $N>1$, the root objective is concave and penalizes losses, so its optimal fraction need not be $1$ even when the mean is positive. For example, if $\mathbb P(R=-1)>0$ and $\mathbb ER>0$, the left <derivative> of $\mathbb E(1+pR)^r$ at $1$ is $-\infty$, while its right <derivative> at $0$ is positive; the optimum lies strictly between $0$ and $1$. If $\mathbb ER\leq0$, <Jensen inequality> gives $\mathbb E(1+pR)^r\leq(1+p\mathbb ER)^r\leq1$, so $p=0$ is optimal. Thus **the root objective can favor partial investment over the all-in policy**.

= 29E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Asymptotic Methods}

= i
{parent=29e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Keep $\nu$ fixed as $x\to+\infty$. On both vertical lines $\sin(\pm\pi+iy)=-i\sinh y$. The right side is traversed upward and the left downward, so their combined contribution is
$$
V(x)=-\frac{\sin(\pi\nu)}\pi\int_0^\infty e^{-x\sinh y-\nu y}\,dy.
$$
Set $v=\sinh y$. The <integral> becomes $\int_0^\infty e^{-xv}a(v)\,dv$, where $a(v)=e^{-\nu\operatorname{arsinh}v}/\sqrt{1+v^2}$, $a(0)=1$ and $a'(0)=-\nu$. Successive <integration by parts>, with exponentially vanishing terms at infinity, gives
$$
\int_0^\infty e^{-xv}a(v)\,dv=\frac1x-\frac\nu{x^2}+O(x^{-3}).
$$
Hence the leading vertical contribution is $\boxed{V(x)=-\sin(\pi\nu)/(\pi x)+O(x^{-2})}$. It is exactly zero for <integer> $\nu$. These estimates hold for fixed real $\nu$; they are not uniform when the order grows with $x$.

= ii
{parent=29e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The horizontal contribution is $B(x)=(2\pi)^{-1}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}e^{-ix\sin z}e^{i\nu z}\,dz$. The two nondegenerate stationary points of the phase $-\sin z$ are $\pi/2$ and $-\pi/2$, with second <derivatives> $1$ and $-1$. The <stationary phase method> gives their contributions
$$
\frac1{\sqrt{2\pi x}}\left(e^{-ix+i\nu\pi/2+i\pi/4}+e^{ix-i\nu\pi/2-i\pi/4}\right)
=\sqrt{\frac2{\pi x}}\cos\left(x-\frac{\nu\pi}2-\frac\pi4\right),
$$
with remainder $O(x^{-3/2})$ for smooth fixed amplitudes localized near the stationary points. On the remaining portions, use <integration by parts>. The endpoint term is
$$
\frac1{2\pi}\left[\frac{e^{-ix\sin z+i\nu z}}{-ix\cos z}\right]_{-\pi}^{\pi}
=\frac{\sin(\pi\nu)}{\pi x}.
$$
Cutoff endpoints cancel between pieces; a second integration gives the other nonstationary contributions $O(x^{-2})$. Thus this endpoint term cancels the leading vertical term. Altogether
$$
\boxed{f_\nu(x)=\sqrt{\frac2{\pi x}}\cos\left(x-\frac{\nu\pi}2-\frac\pi4\right)+O(x^{-3/2}),\qquad a=\frac32.}
$$
This <stationary phase endpoint cancellation in a half-strip contour> is necessary: omitting it would leave an erroneous $x^{-1}$ remainder for noninteger $\nu$.

= 30A
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Dynamical Systems}

= a
{parent=30a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The direct <first Lyapunov theorem> says that a continuously differentiable positive-definite function $V$ near an equilibrium, with $\dot V\leq0$, proves stability. <LaSalle invariance principle> says that in a <compact> positively invariant set, trajectories approach the largest invariant subset of $\{\dot V=0\}$. Under the terminology in which “first theorem” means the linearization theorem, <eigenvalues> with negative real part imply <asymptotic stability>, while zero-real-part <eigenvalues> leave the test inconclusive. Here the linearization has <eigenvalues> $0,-k$, so the direct criterion is needed.

Write $y=\dot x$ and choose
$$
V(x,y)=\frac{y^2}2+\int_0^x\sin^3s\,ds
=\frac{y^2}2+\frac23-\cos x+\frac13\cos^3x.
$$
Near zero, $V=y^2/2+x^4/4+O(x^6)$ is positive definite. Along solutions,
$$
\dot V=y(-ky-\sin^3x)+\sin^3x\,y=\boxed{-ky^2\leq0}.
$$
The component containing the origin of a sufficiently small closed sublevel set is <compact>, lies in $|x|<\pi$ and is positively invariant. The set $\dot V=0$ has $y=0$; to remain there also requires $\sin^3x=0$, so its largest invariant subset is just the origin. The two theorems establish **the origin is asymptotically stable**.

= b
{parent=30a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Poincare-Bendixson theorem> says that a nonempty <compact> omega-limit set of a smooth planar flow containing no equilibrium is a <periodic orbit>; use its allowed cylinder version here. For $F>1$, choose $p_-=(F-1)/(2k)$ and $p_+=2(F+1)/k$. At the lower boundary $\dot p>0$ and at the upper boundary $\dot p<0$, so the <compact> annulus $p_-\leq p\leq p_+$ is positively invariant. It has no equilibrium because $F-\sin\theta>0$. The theorem supplies a <periodic orbit>, and $\dot\theta=p>0$ makes it encircle the cylinder.

Every <periodic orbit> must lie in $p>0$: for $p\leq0$, $\dot p>0$, and a crossing of $p=0$ can only be upward. In $p>0$ a <periodic orbit> is a <graph of a function> satisfying
$$
\frac{dp}{d\theta}=\frac{F-\sin\theta}{p}-k.
$$
Two distinct <function graphs> cannot cross by uniqueness of this scalar differential equation. If $p_1>p_2$, their difference obeys
$$
(p_1-p_2)'=-\frac{F-\sin\theta}{p_1p_2}(p_1-p_2).
$$
It contracts by a factor strictly less than $1$ during one circuit, contradicting periodicity of both <function graphs>. The scalar return map is order preserving, so a multi-circuit periodic <graph of a function> also cannot have a period larger than one circuit. Therefore **there is exactly one <periodic orbit>, and it encircles the cylinder**. The standard auxiliary results used are existence/uniqueness of smooth differential equations and invariance at inward-pointing boundaries.

= c
{parent=30a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For the unforced undamped pendulum, the upper homoclinic separatrix has energy $E=p^2/2-\cos\theta=1$ and $p_0(\theta)=2\cos(\theta/2)$ for $-\pi\leq\theta\leq\pi$. With forcing and damping,
$$
\dot E=Fp-kp^2.
$$
The first-order energy change along this separatrix is
$$
\Delta E=F\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}d\theta-k\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}p_0(\theta)\,d\theta=2\pi F-8k.
$$
The zero of this first-order stable/unstable-manifold splitting is simple in $F$. Smooth dependence of the saddle manifolds and the implicit function theorem therefore give a nearby homoclinic branch with
$$
\boxed{F_h(k)=\frac4\pi k+O(k^2).}
$$
The asymptotic formula is the leading-order stable/unstable-manifold balance. On a lower homoclinic orbit, $p\leq0$ and the lifted angle decreases by $2\pi$, so the exact integrated energy balance would be
$$
0=-2\pi F-k\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}p^2\,dt<0
$$
when $F>0$. Hence **no lower homoclinic orbit exists for positive forcing**.

= 31A
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Integrable Systems}

= Solution
{parent=31A}

Compatibility of the auxiliary system, computed on a fundamental <matrix> of solutions, gives the <zero-curvature condition>
$$
\boxed{U_t-V_x+[U,V]=0.}
$$
Indeed equality of the mixed <derivatives> gives $(U_t+UV-V_x-VU)\Psi=0$. A single possibly zero vector solution would not justify the <matrix> identity; compatibility means a full fundamental family.

Put $H=W_t-VW$. Using the <zero-curvature condition> gives $H_x=UH$. At $x=0$, $W_t=0$ and hence $H(0)=-V(0)$. Uniqueness of the initial-value problem gives $H=-WV(0)$, or $W_t=VW-WV(0)$. Periodicity implies $V(2\pi)=V(0)=v$, so the <monodromy matrix> $w=W(2\pi)$ obeys the <Lax equation>
$$
\boxed{w_t=[v,w].}
$$
Cyclicity of the trace yields $\frac d{dt}\operatorname{tr}(w^k)=k\operatorname{tr}(w^{k-1}[v,w])=0$ for every $k\geq1$.

There is a genuine factor-of-two error in the printed sine-Gordon pair. Assign the second printed <matrix> to $U$ and the first to $V_{\rm print}$:
$$
U=\frac i2\begin{pmatrix}2\lambda&u_x\\u_x&-2\lambda\end{pmatrix},\qquad
V_{\rm print}=\frac1{2i\lambda}\begin{pmatrix}\cos u&-i\sin u\\i\sin u&-\cos u\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Direct multiplication gives
$$
U_t-(V_{\rm print})_x+[U,V_{\rm print}]
=\frac i2\begin{pmatrix}0&u_{xt}-2\sin u\\u_{xt}-2\sin u&0\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Thus **the printed <matrices> encode $u_{xt}=2\sin u$**.

For the stated <sine-Gordon equation>, explicitly repair the first prefactor by taking $V=V_{\rm print}/2$, that is $1/(4i\lambda)$. Its <zero-curvature condition> becomes $u_{xt}=\sin u$. For this corrected pair, $\operatorname{tr}(w(t,\lambda)^k)$ is conserved for every $\lambda\ne0$. Since $U$ is entire in $\lambda$, so are $W$ and these traces. Their Taylor coefficients in $\lambda$ supply an infinite family of <first integrals>, by the identity theorem extending conservation through $\lambda=0$. At a fixed spectral parameter the higher traces of a $2\times2$ <matrix> are related by the <Cayley-Hamilton theorem>; **no independence of the resulting <first integrals> is claimed**.

= 32C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=32C}

Taking $\mathbf n$ along a coordinate axis gives $\sigma_i^2=I$. Taking $\mathbf n=(e_i+e_j)/\sqrt2$ for $i\ne j$ gives $\{\sigma_i,\sigma_j\}=0$. Combining this <anticommutator> relation with the specified <commutator> gives
$$
\sigma_i\sigma_j=\delta_{ij}I+i\epsilon_{ijk}\sigma_k,
$$
and contraction with $a_ib_j$ proves
$$
\boxed{(\mathbf a\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma)(\mathbf b\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma)
=(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b)I+i(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma.}
$$
The operators for <spin angular momentum>, $S_i=\hbar\sigma_i/2$, satisfy $[S_i,S_j]=i\hbar\epsilon_{ijk}S_k$ and $S^2=3\hbar^2I/4$, identifying spin $1/2$. The usual irreducible spin space is two-dimensional; the assumptions also allow repeated copies of it if no Hilbert-space dimension is specified.

Since $\mathbf n\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma$ is Hermitian and squares to $I$, $P_\pm=(I\pm\mathbf n\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma)/2$ are Hermitian, $P_\pm^2=P_\pm$, $P_+P_-=0$ and $P_++P_-=I$. They are its <spectral projectors>. Write
$$
|\chi\rangle=P_+|\chi\rangle+P_-|\chi\rangle.
$$
Each nonzero term is a scalar multiple of a normalized eigenstate with <eigenvalue> $\pm1$. The <Born rule> gives, for normalized $|\chi\rangle$,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(\pm1)=\|P_\pm\chi\|^2=\langle\chi|P_\pm|\chi\rangle=\frac12(1\pm\langle\mathbf n\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma\rangle).}
$$
If $(\mathbf m\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma)|\chi\rangle=|\chi\rangle$, <expectation> of $\{\mathbf m\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma,\mathbf a\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma\}=2\mathbf m\cdot\mathbf a$ gives $\langle\mathbf a\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma\rangle=\mathbf m\cdot\mathbf a$. Taking $\mathbf a=\mathbf n$ yields $\boxed{\mathbb P(\text{up along }\mathbf n)=\tfrac12(1+\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf m)}$.

= 33C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Applications of Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=33C}

For a normalized trial state $|\psi\rangle=\sum_nc_n|n\rangle$ in the form domain of a bounded-below <Hamiltonian>, the <quantum variational principle> gives $\langle H\rangle=\sum_n|c_n|^2E_n\geq E_0$. Thus each trial <expectation> is an upper bound for the ground-state energy. The same spectral argument covers a continuum: for a decaying potential whose essential spectrum begins at $0$, a negative trial <expectation> guarantees a negative-energy <bound state>.

For the stated exponential trial function, normalization follows from $4\alpha^3\int_0^\infty r^2e^{-2\alpha r}\,dr=1$. The kinetic <expectation>, using $\int|\nabla\psi|^2$, is $\hbar^2\alpha^2/(2m)$. The potential <expectation> is $-4A\alpha^3\int_0^\infty re^{-(\mu+2\alpha)r}\,dr$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{E(\alpha)=\frac{\hbar^2\alpha^2}{2m}-\frac{4A\alpha^3}{(\mu+2\alpha)^2}.}
$$
The <exponential variational bound for the Yukawa potential> at $\alpha=\mu/2$ gives $E=(\mu/8)(\hbar^2\mu/m-A)<0$ if $\mu<Am/\hbar^2$. Thus **a <bound state> necessarily exists in the stated range**.

For $\mu\geq Am/\hbar^2$, this single-exponential family cannot prove binding: the maximum of $4A\alpha/(\mu+2\alpha)^2$ is $A/(2\mu)$, so every $E(\alpha)\geq0$. This does not prove absence of <bound states>.

The equality case can in fact be strengthened. At $\mu=Am/\hbar^2$, use normalized exponentials $\psi_{\mu/2},\psi_\mu$ and write $Q=\hbar^2\mu^2/m$. Their <matrix> elements are
$$
\langle\psi_{\mu/2},H\psi_{\mu/2}\rangle=0,\quad
\langle\psi_{\mu/2},H\psi_\mu\rangle=-\frac{8\sqrt2}{675}Q,\quad
\langle\psi_\mu,H\psi_\mu\rangle=\frac Q{18}.
$$
The Rayleigh numerator for $\psi_{\mu/2}+\epsilon\psi_\mu$ is negative for sufficiently small $\epsilon>0$, and its norm is nonzero. Hence **binding also holds at equality, and by <continuity> in a small interval beyond it**. For arbitrary larger $\mu$, these trials alone do not settle existence; a nonnegative variational upper bound cannot establish a nonnegative ground-state energy.

= 34D
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Physics}

= a
{parent=34d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Here $p(n)$ is the <probability> of microstate $n$, including all occupation data. In the <grand canonical ensemble>, with energy $E_n$, particle number $N_n$, <chemical potential> $\mu$ and $T>0$,
$$
p(n)=\mathcal Z^{-1}e^{-(E_n-\mu N_n)/(kT)},\qquad
\mathcal Z=\sum_ne^{-(E_n-\mu N_n)/(kT)}.
$$
Substitution in the <Gibbs entropy> gives $S=k\log\mathcal Z+(U-\mu N)/T$, where $U=\langle E\rangle$ and $N=\langle N_n\rangle$. Differentiating at fixed $\mu,V$ gives $\partial_T\log\mathcal Z=(U-\mu N)/(kT^2)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{S=k\left[\frac{\partial}{\partial T}(T\log\mathcal Z)\right]_{\mu,V}.}
$$

= b
{parent=34d}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For noninteracting <fermions>, each single-particle level has occupation $n_i\in\{0,1\}$ by the <Pauli exclusion principle>. A microstate has $E=\sum_i\epsilon_in_i$, $N=\sum_in_i$, so its grand-canonical weight is the product of its <independent> level weights. The <grand potential> is $\Omega=-kT\log\mathcal Z$; the particle number, <entropy> and <pressure> follow from $N=-\partial_\mu\Omega$, $S=-\partial_T\Omega$ and $P=-\partial_V\Omega$. This occupation description is the basis for the product, continuum and adiabatic calculations.

Summing separately over the two allowed occupations of every level gives
$$
\mathcal Z=\prod_i\sum_{n_i=0}^1e^{-n_i(\epsilon_i-\mu)/(kT)}
=\prod_i\left(1+e^{-(\epsilon_i-\mu)/(kT)}\right).
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\log\mathcal Z=\sum_i\log\left(1+e^{-(\epsilon_i-\mu)/(kT)}\right).}
$$
The factorization uses the absence of interactions; each level, including any degeneracy labels, is counted separately.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Replace the sum by the <integral> against the <density of states> and set $x=\epsilon/(kT)$, $y=\mu/T$:
$$
\log\mathcal Z=AV(kT)^{a+1}\int_0^\infty x^a\log(1+e^{y/k-x})\,dx.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{b=a+1,\qquad f(y)=Ak^{a+1}\int_0^\infty x^a\log(1+e^{y/k-x})\,dx,\qquad\log\mathcal Z=VT^bf(\mu/T).}
$$
For the given $a>0$, the <integral> converges at zero and infinity for every finite $y$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Differentiating the <grand potential> gives
$$
N=kVT^bf'(y),\qquad S=kVT^b[(b+1)f(y)-yf'(y)],\qquad
\frac SN=(b+1)\frac{f(y)}{f'(y)}-y.
$$
A reversible adiabatic change in an isolated gas keeps $S$ and $N$ fixed. To infer constant $y$, one must check that this ratio is one-to-one, rather than merely assuming it.

Put $q=y/k$, $n(x)=1/(1+e^{x-q})$, $F_j=\int_0^\infty x^jn(x)\,dx$ and $M_j=\int_0^\infty x^{a+j}n(x)(1-n(x))\,dx$ for $j=0,1,2$. <Integration by parts> gives $M_1=bF_a$ and $M_2=(b+1)F_{a+1}$, and
$$
\frac S{kN}=\frac{b+1}{b}\frac{F_{a+1}}{F_a}-q,\qquad
\frac d{dq}\frac S{kN}=b\left(1-\frac{M_0M_2}{M_1^2}\right)<0.
$$
The strict inequality is the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> for the positive weight $x^an(x)(1-n(x))$, since $x$ is not constant on its support. This proves <strict monotonicity of Fermi gas entropy per particle> and hence $\mu/T$ is constant. Then $N=kVT^bf'(y)$ gives $VT^b$ constant, and $P=kT^{b+1}f(y)$ gives
$$
\boxed{VT^c=\text{constant},\quad PV^d=\text{constant},\qquad c=a+1,\quad d=\frac{a+2}{a+1}.}
$$
This <adiabatic scaling of a Fermi gas with power-law density of states> concerns positive temperature; the zero-temperature <limit> is a separate limiting case.

= 35D
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=General Relativity}

= a
{parent=35d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $k=1$, put $r=\sin\chi$. The spatial <metric tensor> becomes
$$
a^2\left[d\chi^2+\sin^2\chi(d\theta^2+\sin^2\theta\,d\phi^2)\right].
$$
The global closed spatial slice is a <3-sphere> with $0\leq\chi\leq\pi$; the original $r$ chart covers only a hemisphere, so two charts are needed. A <circle> at fixed $\chi$ in an equatorial angular plane has circumference $\boxed{2\pi a\sin\chi}$. The full spatial volume is
$$
\boxed{4\pi a^3\int_0^\pi\sin^2\chi\,d\chi=2\pi^2a^3.}
$$
Integrating a single $r\in[0,1]$ chart without its second copy would give only half this volume.

= b
{parent=35d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the <Einstein static universe> with $P=0$ and $\rho_0>0$, the acceleration equation gives $\Lambda=4\pi\rho_0$. The first <Friedmann equation> then gives $k/a_0^2=4\pi\rho_0$, so
$$
\boxed{k=1,\qquad a_0=(4\pi\rho_0)^{-1/2},\qquad\Lambda=4\pi\rho_0.}
$$
Keep $\Lambda,k$ fixed and use the specified perturbation closure $\delta P=\delta\rho/3$. Linearization of the constraint gives $\delta\rho=-3\rho_0\delta a/a_0$. In the acceleration equation the background terms cancel, leaving
$$
\delta\ddot a=-\frac{4\pi}3a_0(\delta\rho+3\delta P)
=8\pi\rho_0\delta a=\frac2{a_0^2}\delta a.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\delta a=C_+e^{\sqrt{8\pi\rho_0}\,t}+C_-e^{-\sqrt{8\pi\rho_0}\,t}.}
$$
The growing mode establishes **linear instability of the static universe**; a specially chosen decaying perturbation does not remove instability. This uses the pressure-density relation explicitly supplied for the perturbation. If zero density is allowed, there is also the degenerate vacuum static case $k=0$, $\Lambda=0$, which is not the positive-density static universe considered here.

= 36B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics II}

= Solution
{parent=36B}

Work per unit axial length, with $y$ vertically upward and $x$ across the thin gap. In the frame of the downward-translating cylinder, the wall moves upward at speed $U$ and the cylinder surface is stationary. Let $\eta$ be <viscosity> and use the leading <lubrication theory>
$$
h(y)=h_0+\frac{y^2}{2a},\qquad h_0\ll a.
$$
The transverse <pressure> <gradient> is negligible; solving $\eta u_{xx}=p_y$ with $u(0)=U$, $u(h)=0$ gives
$$
u(x,y)=U\left(1-\frac xh\right)+\frac{p_y}{2\eta}x(x-h),\qquad
q=\int_0^h u\,dx=\frac{Uh}2-\frac{h^3p_y}{12\eta}.
$$
The flux $q$ is constant by <incompressibility>. Equal ambient <pressure> at both ends implies $\int p_y\,dy=0$. With $I_j=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}h^{-j}\,dy$ and $I_2/I_3=4h_0/3$, this yields
$$
q=\frac{2Uh_0}3,\qquad p_y=\frac{6\eta U}{h^2}-\frac{8\eta Uh_0}{h^3},\qquad
\boxed{p(y)=-\frac{2\eta Uy}{h(y)^2}.}
$$
The tangential traction on the cylinder is
$$
\tau=-\eta u_x(h)=-\frac{2\eta U}h+\frac{4\eta Uh_0}{h^2}.
$$
Since $I_2=I_1/(2h_0)$, its integrated force is $-2\eta UI_1+4\eta Uh_0I_2=0$. <Pressure> acts radially on a circular cylinder and contributes no torque; the tangential traction has moment arm $a$ to leading order. Hence
$$
\boxed{F_{\rm tangential}=0,\qquad\text{torque}=0\quad\text{to leading lubrication order}.}
$$
For clarity, the total vertical hydrodynamic force is not zero. Its leading <pressure> contribution is
$$
F_{\rm drag}=-\int p h'\,dy=\boxed{2\pi\eta U\sqrt{\frac{2a}{h_0}}}
$$
upward, while the leading shear contribution just calculated vanishes. Thus **this model predicts no induced rolling direction**: a freely rotating cylinder can remain nonrotating, rather than rolling like a wheel in contact with the wall. This is the <torque-free cylinder in a lubrication gap> cancellation; finite ends or different boundary conditions would require a different model.

= 37B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Waves}

= Solution
{parent=37B}

For isentropic gas with $P=K\rho^\gamma$, $\gamma>1$, the <sound speed> obeys $c^2=P'(\rho)$. The one-dimensional mass and momentum equations are
$$
\rho_t+u\rho_x+\rho u_x=0,\qquad u_t+uu_x+\frac{c^2}{\rho}\rho_x=0.
$$
Since $c\propto\rho^{(\gamma-1)/2}$, these imply
$$
c_t+uc_x+\frac{\gamma-1}2cu_x=0,\qquad
u_t+uu_x+\frac2{\gamma-1}cc_x=0.
$$
Adding and subtracting yields the <Riemann invariants>
$$
\boxed{R_\pm=u\pm\frac{2(c-c_0)}{\gamma-1},\qquad
(\partial_t+(u\pm c)\partial_x)R_\pm=0.}
$$
A <simple wave> has one invariant constant throughout. For a right-moving wave into a resting state with <sound speed> $c_0$, $R_-=0$, so $c=c_0+(\gamma-1)u/2$ and the right characteristic speed is $c_0+bu$, $\boxed{b=(\gamma+1)/2}$.

For the damped scalar equation, characteristics from $\xi$ satisfy
$$
\boxed{u=e^{-\alpha t}u_0(\xi),\qquad
x=c_0t+\xi+\frac b\alpha(1-e^{-\alpha t})u_0(\xi).}
$$
Assume smooth bounded initial data with bounded <derivative>, and the stated maximal compressive slope exists. Then
$$
J=\frac{\partial x}{\partial\xi}=1+\frac b\alpha(1-e^{-\alpha t})u_0'(\xi),\qquad
u_x=\frac{e^{-\alpha t}u_0'(\xi)}J.
$$
For the <damping threshold for a simple wave>, if $m=\max_\xi[-u_0'(\xi)]>0$, put $\boxed{\alpha_c=bm}$. For $0<\alpha<\alpha_c$, a <gradient blow-up> first occurs at
$$
\boxed{t_s=-\frac1\alpha\log\left(1-\frac\alpha{\alpha_c}\right).}
$$
For $\alpha>\alpha_c$, $J$ stays uniformly positive. At $\alpha=\alpha_c$ it still satisfies $J\geq e^{-\alpha t}>0$ for every finite time. At a point attaining the minimum slope, $u_x=u_0'$ for all time, rather than blowing up. Hence the precise conclusion is **no finite-time shock occurs when $\alpha\geq\alpha_c$**, not the printed strict “if and only if $\alpha>\alpha_c$”. Equality only makes the limiting characteristic map degenerate as $t\to\infty$. The profile $u_0(\xi)=-\sin\xi$ directly verifies this endpoint distinction. If there is no compressive slope, take $\alpha_c=0$; no finite-time shock occurs for positive damping. A nonattained maximal slope is interpreted with a supremum and the corresponding infimum of breakdown times. The gas interpretation also requires $c_0+(\gamma-1)u/2>0$.

= 38A
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= i
{parent=38a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Assume sufficient smoothness up to the boundary for uniform Taylor remainders, for example $u\in C^8(\overline\Omega)$ and $f=\Delta u\in C^6(\overline\Omega)$. Direct Taylor expansion of the <nine-point finite-difference stencil> gives
$$
\Gamma_9u=h^2\Delta u+\frac{h^4}{12}\Delta^2u
+\frac{h^6}{360}(u_{xxxxxx}+u_{yyyyyy}+5u_{xxxxyy}+5u_{xxyyyy})+O(h^8).
$$
Thus the normalized <local truncation error> $h^{-2}\Gamma_9u-f$ is $\boxed{O(h^2)}$. The unscaled residual $\Gamma_9u-h^2f$ is $O(h^4)$. Stating the normalization avoids ambiguity in the meaning of truncation order.

= ii
{parent=38a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

If $\Delta f=0$, then $\Delta^2u=0$, so the $h^4$ term in the previous expansion vanishes. Therefore the normalized <local truncation error> is $\boxed{O(h^4)}$, and the unscaled residual is $O(h^6)$. This is generally fourth order under the given harmonic-source condition; special solutions can have additional cancellation, but that is not guaranteed by this argument.

= iii
{parent=38a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <five-point Laplacian> applied to $f$ has expansion
$$
\Gamma_5f=h^2\Delta f+\frac{h^4}{12}(f_{xxxx}+f_{yyyy})+O(h^6).
$$
The corrected right side $h^2f+(h^2/12)\Gamma_5f$ cancels the $h^4\Delta f/12$ term in $\Gamma_9u$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{h^{-2}\left(\Gamma_9u-h^2f-\frac{h^2}{12}\Gamma_5f\right)=O(h^4).}
$$
More precisely, the unscaled residual's $h^6$ coefficient is $-(u_{xxxxxx}+u_{yyyyyy})/240+(u_{xxxxyy}+u_{xxyyyy})/144$. This <fourth-order correction of the nine-point Poisson stencil> does not require $f$ to be harmonic.

= iv
{parent=38a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Let $A$ be the interior-grid <matrix> representing $\Gamma_9$, with zero boundary error. It is real symmetric and has the stated <sine> eigenbasis. Put $s=\sin^2(k\pi h/2)$ and $t=\sin^2(\ell\pi h/2)$; the negative of its <eigenvalue> is
$$
-\lambda_{k\ell}=4s+4t-\frac83st.
$$
This increases in each variable on $[0,1]^2$. With $s_1=\sin^2(\pi h/2)\geq h^2$, the smallest absolute <eigenvalue> is at least $8s_1-(8/3)s_1^2\geq(16/3)h^2$. Hence its <Euclidean norm> <operator norm> satisfies
$$
\|A^{-1}\|_2\leq\frac3{16h^2}.
$$
The corrected scheme has an unscaled residual $r$ whose individual entries are uniformly $O(h^6)$. There are $m^2$ entries and $m<h^{-1}$, so $\|r\|_2=O(h^5)$. Subtracting the exact nodal equations from the numerical equations gives $Ae=-r$ (with sign depending on the residual convention), and therefore
$$
\boxed{\|e\|_2\leq\|A^{-1}\|_2\|r\|_2=O(h^3).}
$$
Choose a constant slightly larger than the resulting bound to obtain the requested strict $\|e\|<ch^3$ for all sufficiently small $h$. Uniform smoothness is needed for an $h$-independent constant; unspecified smoothness only in the interior would not by itself guarantee it.
